   - CVC-ClinicDB: -0.153 Dice below Polyp-PVT (-16.4% relative)
   Even U-Net (2015) beats us on CVC-ClinicDB by 0.04 Dice.

2. OVER-PARAMETERIZED:
   - Our model is 10-12x larger than SOTA (309M vs 25-32M).
   - Bigger backbone (ViT-Large) does NOT translate to better polyp
     segmentation. Larger models overfit with small medical datasets
     and lose the inductive biases (locality, translation-equivariance)
     that CNNs / hierarchical transformers exploit.

3. FPS IS COMPETITIVE (with FP16):
   - 46.6 FPS on dual-T4 with FP16 batching
   - 26.8 FPS on single-T4 FP16 (>= 25 FPS colonoscopy real-time)
   - vs SOTA reported: SSFormer-L ~28, Polyp-PVT ~40, CFA-Net ~55
   -> FPS is acceptable, but accuracy is the blocker.

>>> WHY WE'RE LOSING ACCURACY <<<

a) Wrong architecture family for dense prediction:
   Plain ViT (patch16, no pyramid) produces single-scale features.
   SOTA polyp models (Polyp-PVT, SSFormer, CFA-Net) use HIERARCHICAL
   transformers (PVT/Swin/MiT) with multi-scale features + specialized
   segmentation decoders (RA, CFA, cascaded partial decoders).

b) Weak decode head:
   Our decode_head is a tiny Conv+BN+Deconv+BN+Conv (~2M params). SOTA
   uses Receptive Field Blocks + Reverse Attention modules that are
   published to add ~5-8 Dice points on these datasets.

c) Training data / split unknown:
   CVC-ClinicDB is being evaluated on full 495 images. PraNet standard
   uses 550/62 train/test split. If training used most of 495, the low
   number here means the model didn't even fit the training data --
   under-trained.

d) No polyp-domain augmentation:
   PraNet family uses multi-scale training, color jitter, elastic
   transforms. Vanilla ViT-Large fine-tune is not sufficient.

